 stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Akron's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Akron has set <strong class=\"sig\">20 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 71 that chance alone would give, and <strong>78 record highs</strong>, close to the 71 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (65). Akron's all-time high is 104\u00b0F, on August 6, 1918.","t_chg":"Akron has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 134 complete years: Akron's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.12\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.05 to +0.18), about +1.6\u00b0F since 1888. Akron's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.12\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.05 to +0.18), about +1.6\u00b0F since 1888.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 5 of 12 months. Highs rose in 5 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Akron has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Akron's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.55 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.26 to +0.83), about +7.0 inches since 1897. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 41.5 inches a year. The wettest year was 1990 (65.70 in); the driest, 1963 (23.79 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 2 daily record highs, 2 daily record lows"}}